Structured concurrency.

When you split one task into several concurrent subtasks — fetch a user and their orders at once — you create a scattered family of threads with no natural relationship. If one fails, the others leak on, running pointlessly. If the parent is cancelled, the children keep going. Structured concurrency fixes this by borrowing an idea you already trust from ordinary code: a block scope. Subtasks started together live and die together, inside a scope, as one unit.

Consider the unstructured mess it replaces. With bare executors, concurrent subtasks are independent objects, with no lifecycle tie to each other, or to their parent. You submit a fetch-user task and get a future; you submit a fetch-orders task and get another. Then you call get on the user — and if that throws, the orders task just keeps running; that's a leak. And if fetch-user already failed, you still sit and wait on the orders. Cancellation and error propagation are all manual, and easy to botch.

The idea is to scope subtasks like a code block. Structured concurrency — a recent preview feature, built on virtual threads — says: subtasks forked together should be confined to a scope, and that scope doesn't return until all of them finish. It mirrors how a method call works — you don't leave the block until the work inside it is done. So: try, open-paren, var scope equals new structured-task-scope-shutdown-on-failure, close-paren. Inside, scope-dot-fork of fetch-user starts a subtask; scope-dot-fork of fetch-orders starts another. Then scope-dot-join waits for both. Then scope-dot-throw-if-failed propagates the first failure. And if both succeeded, you return a page built from the two results. Leaving the scope guarantees no subtask is still running.

So what does the scope guarantee? Three things — and this is the payoff. First, an all-or-nothing lifecycle: the scope doesn't exit until every forked subtask has completed, failed, or been cancelled. No orphans, ever — closing the scope cleans up. Second, automatic cancellation: with shutdown-on-failure, the instant one subtask throws, the siblings are cancelled — no wasted work on a result you'll discard. And shutdown-on-success is the mirror: the first success wins, and the rest are cancelled. Third, error propagation that composes: a subtask's failure surfaces at join, or throw-if-failed, in the parent — so one try-catch around the scope handles it. Concurrency errors flow like ordinary ones.

Why does this matter? The deep win is that the structure of your code now matches the structure of the work. Concurrent subtasks are visibly nested inside the operation that owns them — the same way a called method is nested inside its caller. And cancellation, error handling, and cleanup all follow that nesting automatically, instead of being hand-wired across loose futures.

This is the capstone of the module's first half. Virtual threads made one-thread-per-subtask free; structured concurrency gives that a shape — a scope with a lifecycle — so fanning out into concurrent work is as safe and readable as a plain block. Together, they're Java's modern answer to "run several things at once, and treat them as one operation." It's still finalizing across releases, but the model is where Java concurrency is heading. And with the tools for running concurrent work now in hand, the module turns to its harder half: what goes wrong when those threads touch the same data.
